<p>President Trump and senior administration officials addressed thousands at a daylong prayer rally Sunday, May 17, on Washington&#x27;s National Mall. The event was billed as “Rededicate 250: A National Jubilee of Prayer, Praise &amp; Thanksgiving” and as a rededication of the United States as “One Nation under God”. It was tied to celebrations of the 250th anniversary of U.S. independence and organized by Freedom 250, a public-private group working with the White House. Trump appeared in a prerecorded Oval Office video reading from 2 Chronicles. Vice President JD Vance, Secretary of State Marco Rubio, Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and House Speaker Mike Johnson were listed among political participants. Supporters described the gathering as a national prayer and worship event focused on faith, gratitude and the role of Providence in American history. Critics, including church-state separation advocates and some scholars quoted by news organizations, said the event privileged conservative Christianity and blurred constitutional lines between religion and government.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/dzyqTJhNcCtx8458L3690Cvc-9GKx0hW3WDXP1IrT4GI5KyXeUe629_2KqIuei1FJUuzk8H8c0h_xPCqsmQagKPZXuQttmPbC1181NEfpx85duLh0rzHQd09O2mwfKeueREf1PR5CKV33PUUmkH6rQqv2ut23RuIQU2Mr-vuCI7IWR1jelRSsFTeicV5rA4IBDaetL-OmU5Cen3T' alt='People raised their hands in worship during Sunday’s event in Washington, which organizers called “Rededicate 250: A National Jubilee of Prayer, Praise &amp; Thanksgiving.”' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Christian imagery: The stage had arched stained-glass-style windows, columns resembling a federal building and images of founders alongside a white cross.</li><li>Religious lineup: The program featured many evangelical Protestant leaders, with Orthodox Rabbi Meir Soloveichik identified as the only non-Christian religious leader on the roster.</li><li>Public funding: Sveriges Radio reported that the Christian event was partly financed with taxpayer funds.</li><li>Outside protest: Protesters outside the gates objected to the event. Freedom From Religion&#x27;s Annie Laurie Gaylor was quoted as saying the United States should not have a state church.</li></ul><blockquote>If my people, which are called by my name, shall humble themselves, and pray, and seek my face, and turn from their wicked ways, - President Donald Trump</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cardinal Timothy Dolan: Dolan said in a video message that faith in God had been “the bedrock” of American greatness and that the country’s deepest values were rooted in being “a people of God.” (<a href='https://www.ewtnnews.com/world/us/top-u-s-leadership-rededicates-country-as-one-nation-under-god'>EWTN News</a>)</li><li>Sam Perry, Baylor University professor: Perry said linking nationalism with conservative Christian faith was not new, but the event&#x27;s scale and Trump administration leadership stood out. (<a href='https://www.cna.com.tw/news/aopl/202605180041.aspx'>Central News Agency</a>)</li><li>Julie Ingersoll, University of North Florida professor: Ingersoll said the speaker list signaled who organizers considered mainstream Americans and who was left on the margins. (<a href='https://www.cna.com.tw/news/aopl/202605180041.aspx'>Central News Agency</a>)</li><li>Michael Moreland, Villanova University professor: Moreland said public life and religious belief can intersect and called it an overstatement to treat the event as a constitutional problem. (<a href='https://www.nocutnews.co.kr/news/6518554'>No Cut News</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.timesofisrael.com/mass-dc-prayer-event-with-top-white-house-officials-seeks-to-reclaim-us-christian-roots/'>Mass DC prayer event with top White House officials seeks to reclaim US Christian roots</a> - timesofisrael.com</li><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/gallery/2026/5/17/trump-holds-prayer-rally-to-rededicate-us-as-one-nation-under-god'>Trump holds prayer rally to ‘re